nl80211_disconnect_event(): don't push a disconnect into a supplicant
context that was never initialized.

This handler unconditionally forwards every NL80211_CMD_DISCONNECT into
supplicant_event(&interface->wpa_s, EVENT_DISASSOC, NULL), including for
interfaces where no supplicant session ever existed -- interface->wpa_s
is an embedded struct that is zero-initialized with the rest of
wifi_interface_info_t, so its wpa_sm and drv_priv are simply NULL until
a connect actually happens.

hostap's disassoc path then walks straight into those NULLs. Two
separate SIGSEGVs were observed from this one call site, both on the
EasyMesh leaf at the exact moment the controller's WSC M2 fronthaul VAP
configuration was applied and interfaces were being brought down:

  wpa_sm_notify_disassoc     NULL struct wpa_sm *   (crash addr 0x44c)
  wifi_drv_set_operstate     NULL priv              (crash addr 0x562)

  Thread 1 (crashed)
   0  librdk_wifihal.so.0    get_vap_bridge_name
   1  librdk_wifihal.so.0    wifi_drv_set_operstate
   2  libhostap.so.2         wpa_supplicant_set_state
   3  libhostap.so.2         wpa_supplicant_mark_disassoc
   4  libhostap.so.2         wpas_event_disconnect
   5  librdk_wifihal.so.0    nl80211_disconnect_event

The second only became reachable once the first was guarded, which is
the point: fixing this one callee at a time is unbounded, because
wpa_supplicant_mark_disassoc() fans out into a whole chain of helpers
that each assume a live session. wifi_drv_set_operstate() in particular
cannot defend itself meaningfully -- it does

    interface = (wifi_interface_info_t *)priv;
    vap = &interface->vap_info;
    wifi_hal_info_print(..., interface->name, get_vap_bridge_name(vap), ...);

so a NULL priv faults before any check it could contain would run.

Guard at the source instead. The check has to include wpa_s.drv_priv:
that is the pointer wpa_supplicant_set_state() hands to
wifi_drv_set_operstate() as priv, and it is independent of
interface->u.sta.wpa_sm -- a first attempt guarding only on wpa_sm was
observed to still crash here with an identical stack and crash address,
because wpa_sm had been allocated while drv_priv had not. wpa_sm is kept
in the condition as well since it is this file's own marker for "a
supplicant session exists" (the block immediately below uses exactly
that test to decide whether there is anything to tear down), and the
same chain reaches wpa_sm_notify_disassoc(wpa_s->wpa).

The state assignments above it (cancel_auth_timeout, disconnected = 1)
are left unconditional -- they only touch the embedded struct's own
fields and are harmless on a fresh one.

Not hwsim-specific: any interface that sees a DISCONNECT before or
without a completed connect hits this, so it is left ungated.

---
 src/wifi_hal_nl80211_events.c | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/wifi_hal_nl80211_events.c b/wifi_hal_nl80211_events.c
--- a/wifi_hal_nl80211_events.c
+++ b/wifi_hal_nl80211_events.c
@@ -848,11 +848,16 @@
 #if defined(CONFIG_WIFI_EMULATOR) || defined(BANANA_PI_PORT)
     wpa_supplicant_cancel_auth_timeout(&interface->wpa_s);
     interface->wpa_s.disconnected = 1;
+    /* No supplicant session was ever established on this interface, so there is
+     * nothing to disassociate. drv_priv is what wifi_drv_set_operstate() is handed
+     * and dereferences unconditionally, so it is the field that has to be checked. */
+    if (interface->wpa_s.drv_priv != NULL && interface->u.sta.wpa_sm != NULL) {
 #if defined(BANANA_PI_PORT) && (HOSTAPD_VERSION >= 211)
-    supplicant_event(&interface->wpa_s, EVENT_DISASSOC, NULL);
+        supplicant_event(&interface->wpa_s, EVENT_DISASSOC, NULL);
 #else
-    wpa_supplicant_event_wpa(&interface->wpa_s, EVENT_DISASSOC, NULL);
+        wpa_supplicant_event_wpa(&interface->wpa_s, EVENT_DISASSOC, NULL);
 #endif // BANANA_PI_PORT
+    }
 #endif
     if (interface->u.sta.wpa_sm != NULL) {
         eapol_sm_deinit(interface->u.sta.wpa_sm->eapol);
